{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"assay-development-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Assay development track","description":"Develops a new lab assay in gated steps, from intended use and requirements through feasibility, optimisation and validation to an SOP and handover with ongoing quality control.","category":"lab-work","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["design","build","verify","operate"],"role":["researcher","student"],"subject":["biology","chemistry"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","notes","dataset"],"output":["plan","report","docs"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"expert","tags":["assay-development","method-validation","design-of-experiments","quality-control","method-transfer"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["plan-bench-experiment","design-plate-layout","write-lab-protocol","optimize-qpcr-assay","calculate-measurement-uncertainty"],"personas":["lab-manager"]},"args":[{"name":"assay","description":"What the assay should measure, in what sample type, why it is needed, any starting method or kit, and the equipment and reagents available.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"setting","description":"Research use only, or under a regulated or accredited system (clinical, GLP, GMP, ISO/IEC 17025), and who will run the assay routinely. Optional.","type":"text"}],"steps":[{"id":"requirements","file":"steps/01-requirements.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve"},{"id":"feasibility","file":"steps/02-feasibility.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve"},{"id":"optimise","file":"steps/03-optimise.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve"},{"id":"validate","file":"steps/04-validate.md","stage":"verify","gate":"approve"},{"id":"handover","file":"steps/05-handover.md","stage":"operate","gate":"none"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Develops an assay the way an experienced assay scientist would: decide what \"good enough\" means before touching the bench, prove the format can work at all, optimise the factors that matter, validate against the requirements set at the start, and hand over a written method with quality control so results stay trustworthy after the developer leaves.\n\n<assay>\n{{assay}}\n</assay>\n{{#setting}}Setting: {{setting}}{{/setting}}\n\nEach step produces one artifact and stops for approval or the user's bench results; later steps build on the approved versions. Steps 2 to 4 depend on real data: ask for the results and never invent measurements, standard curves or validation figures. If the user wants to continue without data, label the plan as provisional. Mark every unconfirmed value, acceptance limit or regulatory requirement as `[CONFIRM: …]`. Under a regulated or accredited system, say which guideline the validation must follow and that the quality unit approves it; do not claim compliance. If the user asks to skip approvals, confirm once, then run the remaining steps in one reply and state the choice made at each skipped gate.","steps":[{"id":"requirements","file":"steps/01-requirements.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: Requirements\n\nDefine what the assay must do before any bench work.\n\n1. Write the intended use in one or two sentences: the analyte, the sample type, the decision the result supports, and who runs it.\n2. List the performance requirements with target values or [CONFIRM] placeholders: measuring range, required sensitivity (limit of detection or quantitation), precision, accuracy or recovery, specificity against likely interferents, sample volume, throughput, time to result and cost per sample.\n3. Say which requirements are must-have and which are nice-to-have, and which guideline governs validation if the setting is regulated.\n4. List candidate assay formats with a one-line case for each, and the main risk of each.\n5. List the questions the user must answer before feasibility work.\n\nStop and wait for approval of the requirements and the shortlisted formats."},{"id":"feasibility","file":"steps/02-feasibility.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Feasibility\n\nFind out whether the chosen format can work at all, cheaply.\n\n1. Design a small pilot for the chosen format: the minimum conditions, the positive and negative samples, blanks, and replicates needed to see a signal-to-background difference.\n2. State the go, rethink and stop criteria for the pilot before it runs, tied to the requirements from step 1.\n3. List materials and what can be borrowed or reused before buying.\n4. Ask for the pilot results. When they arrive, interpret them against the criteria and recommend go, change format or stop.\n\nStop and wait for the pilot results and the go decision. Do not plan optimisation before then."},{"id":"optimise","file":"steps/03-optimise.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: Optimise\n\nTune the factors that matter, with as few runs as possible.\n\n1. List the candidate factors (for example reagent concentrations, incubation times and temperatures, sample dilution, blocking, wash steps) and rank them by expected effect from the pilot.\n2. Choose the approach: one factor at a time for two or three factors, or a screening design of experiments followed by a response surface for more, with the reason. Lay out the runs, including centre points and replicates, and suggest plate layouts where relevant.\n3. Define the response to optimise (signal-to-background, a robustness measure such as Z-prime for screening assays, precision) and the trade-offs.\n4. Ask for the results, then interpret them, pick the operating conditions and note any factor the assay is sensitive to, which becomes a robustness check in validation.\n\nStop and wait for approval of the final conditions."},{"id":"validate","file":"steps/04-validate.md","stage":"verify","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 4: Validate\n\nShow that the assay meets the requirements from step 1.\n\n1. Write the validation plan: characteristics to test (precision within run and between runs, days and operators; accuracy or recovery; linearity and range; limit of detection and quantitation; specificity and interference; robustness for the sensitive factors; sample stability if relevant), with the experiment design and acceptance criteria for each, referring to the governing guideline if the setting is regulated.\n2. Ask for the data. When it arrives, evaluate each characteristic against its criterion, showing the calculations, and list any failures with the likely cause.\n3. Write the validation summary: what passed, what failed, any restrictions on use (for example a narrower range), and the measurement uncertainty if required.\n\nStop and wait for approval of the validation summary. Do not write the SOP until validation passes or its restrictions are accepted."},{"id":"handover","file":"steps/05-handover.md","stage":"operate","gate":"none","text":"# Step 5: SOP and handover\n\nMake the assay something others can run reliably.\n\n1. Write the SOP outline from the validated conditions: scope, materials with grades, preparation, numbered steps with critical points, calculations, acceptance criteria for each run, and troubleshooting.\n2. Set up ongoing quality control: control materials at two or more levels, run acceptance rules, control charts and what to do when a rule is broken.\n3. Plan the transfer to routine users: training, a comparison run by the new operators against the developer's results, and sign-off.\n4. List what to record for reagent lot changes and when partial revalidation is needed.\n\nThis is the last step."}]}
